 between two fixed ports (MCP_PORT_A=8092/MCP_PORT_B=8093) chosen off the live-serving port, but the retention model permits up to MCP_RELEASES_CAP=3 concurrently-managed containers when busy ones can't be reclaimed. A third overlapping container has no free port, so `docker run -d -p 127.0.0.1:<port>:8091` collides with an already-bound A/B port and the deploy fails — in exactly the never-idle-saturation scenario blue-green was chosen to survive.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f0c83f3eb8404768b","impact":0.8,"is_primary":null,"location":"Run mechanism + the original compose container (bootstrap); Approach step 6 (MCP_RELEASES_CAP)","norm_id":"nf687e4b158a474d1","priority":0.7,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Two agents keep two successive OLD rebar-mcp containers with in_flight>0 (the 7b4a saturation case); a new source change tries to start a third container and the only remaining A/B port is already bound.","Rapid back-to-back MCP source changes while a certified op is long-running on the just-retired-but-still-busy container."],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Choose the new host port by scanning a port range sized to MCP_RELEASES_CAP for the first unbound port (skipping ports held by any managed container), rather than toggling A/B off the live port; if none is free, emit AUTODEPLOY_MCP_RETIRE_CAP (or a distinct marker) and abort without a bind failure.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.875,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified"